302 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Too often\054 design principles fall into the trap of\240sounding good\240without actually\240being useful\056 \n\nWhen principles read like truisms\n\n \034AI should be trustworthy\054\035 \034prioritize the user\054\035 \034support human judgment\035\n\nthey\031re hard to disagree with\054 but even harder to\240design from\056 That\031s why we took a different approach\056\n\nInstead of treating principles as declarations of truth\054 we treated them as\240reasoned tradeoffs\056 We wrote each principle so that its\240opposite is also plausible\024something a smart\054 thoughtful team\240might\240believe\054 in a different context\054 or with different goals\056 This forces us to confront the nuance behind real design choices\056 It pushes us past default thinking and asks\072\240What are we really optimizing for\077 And why\077\n\nWhat this approach looks like\072\n\nEach principle takes a\240clear stance\240with a\240credible counterpoint\056\nThe principle and its opposite both\240hold water\024but imply different values\054 priorities\054 or assumptions\056\nThe goal isn\031t to win a debate\054 but to clarify the lens through which we\031re designing\056)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 303 0 R
     /Pg 296 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

